  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Bell Textron has $1.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Bell Textron received 686 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $2.8M, and obligations are up 26.0% year over year. Bell Textron’s obligations rose steadily from FY21 through FY26: $349M in FY21, $307M in FY22, $985M in FY23, $1.2B in FY24, $1.5B in FY25, and $1.9B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $1.9B to Bell Textron in FY2026 to date. The Department of the Interior follows with $53.2M, while smaller amounts come from the Department of Justice ($278K), the Department of Transportation ($88.9K), and the Department of Energy ($58.5K). Aircraft Manufacturing is Bell Textron’s largest market with $1.8B in obligations, supported by Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $157M. Several small technical and manufacturing markets round out activity, including Scale and Balance Manufacturing at $1.4M and Engineering Services at $635K. This profile reflects concentrated defense aircraft work alongside a mix of parts, services, and niche manufacturing contracts.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have Bell Textron's federal obligations changed over time?",
        "body": "Bell Textron’s obligations climbed from $349M in FY21 to $1.9B in FY26. Bell Textron recorded $307M in FY22, $985M in FY23, $1.2B in FY24, and $1.5B in FY25. Bell Textron’s obligations are up 26.0% year over year in FY2026 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are Bell Textron's top federal customers and markets?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is Bell Textron’s top customer with $1.9B in obligations, followed by the Department of the Interior with $53.2M. Aircraft Manufacturing is the largest market for Bell Textron at $1.8B, with Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $157M and Scale and Balance Manufacturing at $1.4M rounding out the leading industry categories."
      }
    ]
